## Decision Framework
The campaign target mixes two very different kinds of criteria. Weight them differently:

### Hard reject — say no only when one of these clearly holds
- An explicit hard exclusion in the target (e.g. "must NOT be based in X", "never contact Y", a specifically banned category).
- The company is stealth or unlaunched — no public website, product in private beta, or "stealth" mode. There is nothing to reach and no discoverable contact.
- The person has no decision-making authority and no plausible path to it (an intern, a pure individual contributor, a gatekeeper with no influence).
- A pure freelancer with no company behind them. A founder/CEO whose company happens to do services or consulting is NOT a freelancer — they run a company.

### Soft criteria — signals to weigh, never alone a reason to reject
Headcount band, industry niche, company type, website polish, number of vendors, country (unless an explicit hard exclusion), exact title wording, product stage, and any criterion the target merely prefers without a hard "must NOT". A strong founder who misses one or two of these is still a good prospect.

### The borderline rule — give them a chance
If the prospect is a founder/CEO/decision-maker of a live, public-facing company but does not literally fit every clause of the target: **qualify them**. Report the fit and the caveat honestly in your reason — what matches, what doesn't, and why they are still worth a first email. Only reject when a hard-reject condition above clearly holds; when in doubt, qualify.
